#!/usr/bin/env python3
# mac/usage/mac-usage - what the Mac Studio does, and what that would have
# cost on hosted Macs. Data to decide whether it pays its way.
#
#   mac-usage sample          root, every minute (launchd daemon): one host
#                             snapshot, appended to /var/log/mac-usage
#   mac-usage collect         worker, every 15 minutes (launchd agent): CI jobs
#                             from GitHub and agent sessions from Claude Code's
#                             transcripts, then writes the summary
#   mac-usage report [--since YYYY-MM-DD] [--json]
#                             anyone: prints the summary
#
# What each measures, and its limits (docs/mac-studio.md, "Usage and cost"):
# - CI: every job of every cut-watch repo from GitHub's Actions API, with the
#   runner it ran on. Minutes are rounded up per job, as GitHub bills them.
#   Queue wait is the job's start minus its creation. The first collect
#   backfills from BACKFILL_FROM.
# - Agent sessions: the timestamps in worker's ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl.
#   A session is active between two entries no more than IDLE_GAP_SECONDS
#   apart. Only timestamps and the folder name are read, never the text.
# - Host: load, CPU, memory, swap, memory pressure, free disk, and the CPU and
#   memory of the VMs, Claude, Xcode and the simulators, from ps, top,
#   vm_stat and sysctl. Power and thermal pressure from powermetrics: CPU, GPU
#   and ANE power only, so a floor under the power at the wall. No history
#   before the daemon first ran.
#
# The prices and the purchase are constants, not settings, for the same reason
# as monitoring/report.go's thresholds: they are the judgement the summary
# exists to apply. Check them when they change.
#
# Python 3 standard library only. Nothing here sends data anywhere but
# GitHub's API, which it only reads.

import argparse
import datetime as dt
import glob
import json
import math
import os
import re
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import urllib.error
